In Linux, a daemon (pronounced "DEE-mən") is a long-running background process that operates independently of an interactive user. Unlike standard desktop applications or terminal tools, daemons do not have a graphical interface, nor are they attached to a controlling terminal window.
They act as the system's "invisible workers," quietly waiting for specific events, schedules, or requests to perform critical system tasks.


Key Characteristics of a Daemon

• No User Interaction: They start, run, and stop without needing direct human input.
• The "d" Suffix: By convention, most daemon program names end with the letter d (e.g., sshd, syslogd, crond).
• Detached from Terminals: If you close your terminal window, open applications usually shut down. Daemons explicitly sever their link to the terminal so they keep running safely in the background.
• Boot Initiation: Many daemons are triggered automatically when the system boots up and run continuously until the machine is turned off.


Common Examples in Linux

• sshd (Secure Shell Daemon): Listens for incoming remote connection requests so you can log into the machine securely.
• crond (Cron Daemon): Monitors the system clock to execute scheduled background tasks (like automated backups at midnight).
• httpd / nginx: Web server daemons that listen for network requests to serve webpages to internet browsers.
• systemd: The very first process that starts when Linux boots up; it acts as the "parent" daemon responsible for launching and managing all other daemons.

How to View and Manage Daemons

Modern Linux distributions use a system manager called systemd to control these background processes using the systemctl command.

systemctl status sshd
sudo systemctl stop sshd
sudo systemctl start sshd

Enable a daemon to start automatically at boot
sudo systemctl enable sshd

ps aux | grep 'd '
